Description: command runner whose task list is a Guile Scheme file
 Maak runs a project's automation from a Maak file: an ordinary Guile Scheme
 module in which every procedure is a task.  The docstring is the task's
 documentation, the lambda list is its argument grammar, and the command a
 task runs is an argv list rather than a shell string, so a path with a space
 in it stays one argument.
 .
 The same file is read, rather than run, to answer a question about it, so a
 listing, a dry run, a plan of the commands and the call graph work on a file
 somebody is halfway through writing.  Tasks compose like any other
 procedure, run in parallel with -j, and may be pinned to a Guix or Nix store
 path.  Like make in spirit, it takes a real programming language instead of
 a domain specific one.
